<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banat is a historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and Mureș rivers and the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>. Part of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1552, was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1716 and run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as a frontier and colonisation zone. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banat.jpg" alt="Banat" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Turbojet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timisoara,_Piata_Unirii,_flancul_sudic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
